<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">5 January in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">6 events that happened on 5 January, from 1066 to 1968.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1066</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Edward the Confessor dies, leaving England's throne disputed</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">King Edward died at Westminster without children. Rival claims by Harold Godwinson, Harald Hardrada and William of Normandy led to two invasions and the Norman Conquest that autumn.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1477</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Charles the Bold of Burgundy killed at the Battle of Nancy</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Duke Charles the Bold died fighting Swiss and Lorrainer troops outside Nancy. His death ended Burgundian ambitions to become a kingdom, and his lands were divided between France and the Habsburgs.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1757</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Robert-François Damiens attempts to assassinate Louis XV</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">At Versailles, Damiens stabbed Louis XV with a penknife, causing only a slight wound. He was later executed with notorious cruelty, the last person in France to be put to death by quartering.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1919</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">German Workers' Party founded in Munich</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Railway locksmith Anton Drexler founded the small nationalist German Workers' Party. Adolf Hitler joined that autumn and in 1920 the party was renamed the National Socialist German Workers' Party.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1933</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Construction of the Golden Gate Bridge begins</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Work began on a bridge across the Golden Gate strait at the entrance to San Francisco Bay. Opened in 1937, its 1,280-metre main span was the longest in the world until 1964.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1968</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Alexander Dubček takes charge in Czechoslovakia</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Dubček replaced Antonín Novotný as leader of the Czechoslovak Communist Party. His reforms, 'socialism with a human face', inspired the Prague Spring, ended by a Warsaw Pact invasion in August.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a></div></li></ol>
